Practical No. 7
Title
Implementation of Student Registration Form using Angular Reactive Forms, Validation, and
Backend API Submission
Aim
To create a simple Angular application using Reactive Forms Module to demonstrate form
creation, validation, error handling, and submitting form data to backend APIs.
Software Requirements
● Node.js
● Angular CLI
● Web Browser
Theory
Template-driven Forms
Template-driven forms are created using HTML templates and use the ngModel directive.
They are suitable for simple forms but provide less control.
Reactive Forms
Reactive forms are created in the component using TypeScript. They provide better
validation, scalability, and control over form data.
Reactive Forms Module
The ReactiveFormsModule is required to build reactive forms in Angular.
Form Validation
Form validation ensures that user input is correct before submitting the form. Angular
provides built-in validators such as required and email.
Error Handling
Error handling is implemented by displaying validation error messages and handling HTTP
errors during form submission.
Submitting Form Data
Angular uses HttpClient to send form data to backend APIs using HTTP methods like
POST.
Procedure
1. Create a new Angular project.
2. Import ReactiveFormsModule in AppModule.
3. Create a component for the student form.
4. Define form structure using FormGroup and FormControl.
5. Apply validation rules using Validators.
6. Display validation error messages.
7. Submit form data to backend API using HttpClient.
8. Display success or error message.
Program Code (Minimum – Exam Oriented)
1. app.module.ts
import { ReactiveFormsModule } from '@angular/forms';
import { HttpClientModule } from '@angular/common/http';
@NgModule({
imports: [
ReactiveFormsModule,
HttpClientModule
]
})
export class AppModule { }

2. student-form.component.ts
import { FormGroup, FormControl, Validators } from '@angular/forms';
import { StudentService } from '../student.service';
studentForm = new FormGroup({
name: new FormControl('', Validators.required),
email: new FormControl('', [Validators.required, Validators.email]),
course: new FormControl('', Validators.required)
});
constructor(private studentService: StudentService) {}
onSubmit() {
this.studentService.addStudent(this.studentForm.value)
.subscribe({
next: res => alert('Form Submitted Successfully'),
error: err => alert('Submission Error')
});
}

3. student-form.component.html
<form [formGroup]="studentForm" (ngSubmit)="onSubmit()">
Name:
<input type="text" formControlName="name"><br><br>
Email:
<input type="email" formControlName="email"><br><br>
Course:
<input type="text" formControlName="course"><br><br>
<button type="submit" [disabled]="studentForm.invalid">
Submit
</button>
</form>

4. student.service.ts
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class StudentService {
apiUrl = 'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts';
constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}
addStudent(data: any) {
return this.http.post(this.apiUrl, data);
}
}

